# ti.cc2640r2f.gpio_uart_i2c_demo
# Real, hand-written (no vendor SDK/generator - see target.toml) bare-metal
# GPIO + UART0 + I2C0 demo for TI SimpleLink CC2640R2F, the third TI chip
# under this plan, first SimpleLink-family one. See
# ../../../../../replica-server/renoly_examples/ti/cc2640r2f/README.md.

using sysbus
mach create "CC2640R2F_GPIO_UART_I2C_Demo"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platform.repl

# Bare @$ORIGIN/firmware.elf, not a path reflecting the local build-output
# layout - REQUIRED, not stylistic. server/bundle.py's materialize_bundle
# only literal-string-replaces exactly that bare form when staging an
# uploaded bundle for the real client -> StartSession/RunSimulation path;
# anything else resolves fine for local direct-parse testing but silently
# fails to load over the real deployed path. A synced flat firmware.elf
# copy lives at this demo's own root (sibling to this file).
sysbus LoadELF @$ORIGIN/firmware.elf

# UART0 DR write watchpoint - observes the boot marker as it's transmitted
# (real, timing-independent proof it actually ran). "[Tag] Description"
# convention (see server/session.py's WatchpointHook.label/description) -
# is_byte_tap (chr(value) matches) routes this to Bus Traffic, matching
# every other UART-touching example in this repo, not Events.
sysbus AddWatchpointHook 0x40001000 4 Write "print(chr(value & 0xFF), end='')"

# GPIO DOUTTGL31_0 write watchpoint, firing only when the real LED bit
# (DIO7, 0x80) is set - direct proof the blink loop genuinely toggles the
# real onboard LED pin, not just that main() didn't fault. This register
# covers all 32 DIOs at one fixed address (the real, genuinely different
# unified-GPIO design - see gpio.py's own docstring), unlike Stellaris/
# Tiva's per-port masked-DATA range. Not a byte-tap -> lands as a real,
# labeled [GPIO] Event.
sysbus AddWatchpointHook 0x400220B0 4 Write "if value & 0x80: print('[GPIO] DIO7 toggle value=0x%X' % (value & 0x80))"

# I2C0 MCTRL (0x40002804) write watchpoint, firing only when the real
# RUN|START bits (0x1|0x2=0x3) are set - direct proof the I2C0 bus-scan
# loop genuinely issued a START, not just that main() didn't hang. Not a
# byte-tap -> lands as an [I2C0] Event.
sysbus AddWatchpointHook 0x40002804 4 Write "if value & 0x3: print('[I2C0] MCTRL START condition')"

start
